One guarded path for anyone posting a review reply, human or agent

Adds three small pure helpers to the gbp-reviews plugin and wires two of them into the worker. hostScope.ts reads the company and user the host stamped onto params.hostScope (and only that), refusing with an [ESCOPE] error when the scope is missing or has no company, so no handler can be scoped by something the browser wrote. draftReply.ts takes the suggested-reply template out of worker.ts and fixes it: it now takes the numeric 1 to 5 rating the table stores (the old version expected Google's ONE..FIVE strings, so every stored review would have been offered the apology), drops the 60-character splice of the customer's complaint, replaces the em-dash with a comma, and adds postsAs(location, account) for the 'Posts as: <location>, using the Google account <email or name or key>' line. replySource.ts decides at sync time whether a reply still came from Paperclip (text unchanged keeps human or agent) or should be marked as Google's (changed, or first seen). worker.ts calls the moved template with starRatingToNumber and the daily sync's UPDATE now also sets reply_source. That column arrives with migration 002 in the next item, so this change should ship with it. 22 new tests, 43 total passing, typecheck and build clean.

Adds the single guarded path by which a Google review reply is posted, shared by the agent tool now and by the Reviews page in the next items. Migration 002 adds reply_source to the reviews table and a reply_posts audit table with the idempotency key as primary key and a partial unique index allowing one in-flight attempt per review. replyStore.ts wraps the two tables behind a small interface (find a post, find the unsettled one, list every unsettled one, begin, record what is about to be replaced, finish, upsert the review, read the review row) so the decision chain can be tested with an in-memory copy; it turns the two unique violations into plain results and lets a retry with the same key restart a failed or unknown row, but only when that row is for the same review and the same company, and only a failed row may carry different text. replyGuard.ts runs the checks in this order: host-checked company, the allowReplies switch, a well-formed review name, a location that belongs to this very company (one message whether missing or another company's), text of 1 to 4096 characters, the idempotency key's own history, and somebody else's unsettled attempt on the same review (one still posting and recently touched means wait; an older one is marked abandoned so the slot frees, and either way an unsettled row is set aside to be checked against Google below). Only then does it claim the slot in the database, before Google is read rather than after: the partial unique index allows one attempt in flight per review, and holding it from before the read to after the write is what stops two attempts both seeing no reply and both posting, the second silently replacing the first. With the slot held it fetches the Google client, reads the live review, settles any attempt it set aside earlier against what Google actually holds, applies the overwrite rule (a reply already on Google is never replaced by an agent and only by a person who was shown it and whose expected update time still matches), records the reply it is about to replace, makes the one PUT, and finally writes the outcome and the local upsert with the reply's source. Every way out after the claim leaves the row settled: a refusal or a thrown error marks it failed and releases the slot, so a review is never blocked by its own safety check. An attempt's age is measured from when its row was last touched rather than from when it was created, because a retry restarts the row in place and would otherwise look abandoned the moment it started. An attempt key is bound to the review and company it was first used for, so a reused key is refused rather than restarting the first review's audit row while the write goes to a second one. A lost connection marks the row unknown and the retry checks Google before deciding, so nothing posts twice; and because an unsettled row hides the Post button, settlePendingAttempts reconciles every lost attempt on a review against Google whenever the page reads that review, so no review can be blocked for good by an attempt whose key nobody holds any more. worker.ts rewires gbp_reply_to_review through the guard with source 'agent', replaceExisting false, idempotency key run:<runId>:<reviewId> and the person behind the run when there is one, so an agent run a person started names them in the audit row too; refusals return the tool's failure shape. 39 new tests (82 in the plugin), typecheck and build clean, and the host's own SQL validators accept every migration and runtime statement.

Adds the worker side of the Reviews page to the gbp-reviews plugin. A new reviewQueries.ts holds the page's reads, each constrained on the location key AND the location's own company id (never the viewing company), which lets HQ read every location and stops a row filed under the wrong company from ever being shown or counted; it also decides which location a company may open (resolveScopedLocation, by key, and resolveScopedLocationForReview, by the ids inside a parsed review name) and whether posting is allowed from there (own company only, never the roll-up). worker.ts now starts every page handler with the host-stamped scope (requireCompanyScope) and refuses with [ESCOPE] when it is missing, so the companyId the browser sends is ignored; review-summary reads the stamp only and its SQL gains the company_id condition. Four handlers are added: review-list (location, account label, last synced time, canPostFromHere, reviews newest first with unreplied on top), review-detail (the stored row plus a live read from Google, the suggested reply, the 'Posts as' line, the allowReplies switch and two separate flags for whether the location's Google account is in the settings at all and whether it is allowed for this company, any pending attempt, and the issue id; a failed Google read is reported, not thrown, and after a successful one every lost attempt on that review is settled against what Google holds, which is how a review that an abandoned attempt was hiding the Post button on gets its way back), review-post-reply (refuses HQ with [EROLLUP_READ_ONLY], then runs the shared postReplyGuarded with source 'human' and the host-stamped user, throwing refusals so the bridge reports failure), and review-sync-location (own company only, reuses syncLocationReviews and returns counts). The review issue's instruction line now says to open Reviews inside the company and press Sync now instead of commenting '@CEO Agent reply approved'; the issue title, issue body, digest and tool result strings lose their long dashes; the digest query is also company-constrained. A one-character fix to wrapGbpError lets Google HTTP error codes through unwrapped. The sync was made honest at the same time: an existing row now takes its location key and company from the location it is being synced under, so a location repointed at another company does not leave its rows filed under the old one where no screen can see them; a newly inserted row records where its reply came from instead of leaving that blank; a reply whose text has changed is only credited to Google after the audit table has been asked whether one of our own finished attempts sent exactly that text, so a reply Paperclip posted but could not record locally is not later credited to Google; and each location's last sync time is now recorded by the sync itself, so the page shows when Google was last read rather than when a row last changed, and a location that has never been synced says so. The two remaining agent tools, gbp_sync_location and gbp_list_reviews, now refuse a location that is not this company's before any Google call, which the reply and get tools always did. 50 new tests (132 in the plugin), typecheck and build clean, and the host's SQL validator accepts every new statement.

Three items in one commit on purpose: the sync now writes a reply_source column that the migration in the same change creates, so they are not independently releasable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
